Forager patch 0007: the barriers layer's zoom-15 mark is a fill over the tile, not a point, so Planetiler still
deduplicates tiles (dispatch 2026-11-01-coder-09, after run usca-20261031T193102Z ran out of heap).

Against protomaps/basemaps at ca93fc06efbaff1c5f069d06edbe5de18839c1c8 (tiles 4.15.2), with 0001 to 0006 already
applied. Applied after them with `git apply` by map-build/bin/build-basemap-jar.sh.

Planetiler 0.10.2 hashes and deduplicates a tile only when every feature in it is a polygon fill or a tile edge
(VectorTile.likelyToBeDuplicated). 0004's mark, a point at the tile's corner, made every zoom-15 tile fail that test, so
tiles of open water or forest interior were each written and listed separately. On Oregon the archive's tile entries
grew 46.8 % (418,172 to 613,976) and its contents 58.4 %; at US + Canada scale the writer's in-heap entry list grew by
about 1.2 GB, and the 10-31 run ran out of its 4 GB heap at 48 % of the archive.

The mark becomes one polygon over the whole tile and its 4 px buffer, with no id and no fields, still added to every
zoom-15 tile by Barriers' TilePostProcessor. A tile of only fills plus the mark stays one Planetiler deduplicates. The
app's reader takes only lines from this layer (MapFeatures, PR #439), so it ignores the mark either way; a tile with
the layer still means fences were checked. On Oregon: 418,177 entries and 434.8 MB, against 613,979 and 458.5 MB.

Pinned by BarriersTest: the mark is a fill (foragerMarkOnAZoom15TileWithNoBarrier), and a tile of only fills with the
mark is still likelyToBeDuplicated (foragerMarkKeepsAFillOnlyTileDeduplicated, new; 9 forager cases in all).

